ions of southeast\nUtah and southwest Colorado from Sunday morning through Monday\nmorning over soils that are still saturated from recent rainfall\nand flooding.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T11:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Southwest San Juan Mountains; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.28dceea107844d87e801d5ba537294d1bcbe9f7e.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.28dceea107844d87e801d5ba537294d1bcbe9f7e.001.1"},{"event":"Avalanche Advisory","headline":"Avalanche Advisory issued October 31 at 7:16PM MDT by NWS Denver CO","description":"SABCO\n\nTHE FOLLOWING MESSAGE IS TRANSMITTED AT THE REQUEST OF THE\nCOLORADO AVALANCHE INFORMATION CENTER.\n\n...A SPECIAL AVALANCHE ADVISORY IN EFFECT FROM 4:30 PM SATURDAY\nUNTIL 4:30 PM MONDAY...\n\n*WHAT...Avalanche conditions are dangerous above treeline.\n*WHERE...West Elk Mountains, Elk Mountains, San Juan Mountains, and\nLa Plata Mountains\n*WHEN...From 4:30 PM Saturday until 4:30 PM Monday.\n*IMPACTS...Backcountry travelers can easily trigger dangerous\navalanches large enough to bury a human.\n\n*PRECAUTION/PREPAREDNESS ACTIONS...A potent winter storm increases\navalanche danger Sunday into Monday. Rain, snow, and wind will cause\nnatural avalanches above treeline. Check the forecast at\nwww.colorado.gov/avalanche.\n\nBeyer & Pritchett","ends":"2026-11-02T16:30:00-07:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"West Elk and Sawatch Mountains; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df58147db243f.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df58147db243f.001.1"},{"event":"Winter Storm Warning","headline":"Winter Storm Warning issued October 31 at 1